{"id":"n-20261030-apszhu","category":"travel","display_title":"Hiking Snowdonia Slate Trail","display_title_he":"טיול בשביל הצפחות של סנודוניה","short_summary":"A travel feature exploring the 83-mile Snowdonia Slate Trail in Wales, highlighting abandoned industrial heritage, dramatic mountain scenery, and local history.","english_level":"C1","words":433,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/travel/2026/oct/29/snowdonia-slate-trail-walk-north-wales","title":"‘Looming stone towers like Maya temples’: a hike through Snowdonia’s slate country","published_at":"2026-10-29T07:00:25.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-29T07:00:25.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-30T07:52:56.876Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["For travelers seeking a blend of rugged wilderness and industrial heritage, the Snowdonia Slate Trail offers a remarkable journey through the heart of Wales. Spanning 83 miles in a grand loop across the national park, this long-distance route connects a series of abandoned slate quarries with some of the finest riverside and mountain walking paths in the United Kingdom. Hikers traversing the trail encounter towering stone structures, deep crater lakes, and the remnants of a once-booming industrial landscape that shaped the region.","The historical roots of the area run deep. While the exceptional quality of local slate was recognized early on—with Edward I believed to have used it to roof his Welsh castles—large-scale industrial production truly began in the 1820s and peaked during the 1870s. At its height, the terrain bristled with steam pumps, railways, and aerial cables. Among the most striking sites along the route is the Dorothea quarry, a vast abandoned complex featuring huge stone towers constructed without mortar, which now stand overgrown with birch, ash, and oak trees.","As the trail winds further into the mountains, it passes near Yr Wyddfa, also known as Snowdon, before heading south along the River Glaslyn. Adventurous hikers often take detours up dramatic peaks like the Matterhorn-shaped Cnicht, where sweeping views of the Irish Sea reward the climb, though unpredictable weather can bring sudden hailstones and howling winds. Descending from the heights leads into Cwmorthin, an epic quarry dominated by vast piles of waste stone, requiring ten tons of material to produce just one ton of marketable slate. During the late 19th century, this specific site earned the grim local moniker of 'the slaughterhouse' because of the high frequency of fatal accidents among the workforce.","The harsh working conditions, poor pay, and repeated failure of labor strikes ultimately drove many local quarrymen to emigrate to the United States in search of better livelihoods. Today, the abandoned cottages and rustic barracks where miners once lived serve as quiet reminders of their resilience. Further along the route, hikers encounter natural wonders such as the cascading River Cynfal, ancient moss-covered gorges, and historical landmarks including the Church of St Tudclud, which houses the Carausius Stone—believed to be the gravestone of a Roman commander who pulled off a coup d'etat in AD 286 to become emperor of Britain and northern Gaul.","Concluding near the historic village of Betws-y-Coed and the scenic River Conwy, the trail provides a profound connection to the past. Walkers seeking a self-guided adventure over seven days can experience the route, immersing themselves in the dramatic topography and rich history of the Welsh mountains."],"facts":[{"fact":"The Snowdonia Slate Trail is an 83-mile hiking route that loops around the national park in Wales.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The trail passes through abandoned slate quarries such as Dorothea, Cwmorthin, and Rhiwbach.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Industrial slate production in the area began in the 1820s and reached its peak in the 1870s.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Cwmorthin quarry was historically known as 'the slaughterhouse' due to the high number of fatal accidents.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Many Welsh slate workers eventually emigrated to the United States after poor pay and industrial disputes.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"travelers","lemma":"traveler","pos":"noun","he":"מטיילים","name":false},{"text":"seeking","lemma":"seek","pos":"verb","he":"מחפשים","name":false},{"text":"blend","lemma":"blend","pos":"noun","he":"שילוב","name":false},{"text":"rugged","lemma":"rugged","pos":"adjective","he":"מחוספס","name":false},{"text":"wilderness","lemma":"wilderness","pos":"noun","he":"שממה","name":false},{"text":"industrial","lemma":"industrial","pos":"adjective","he":"תעשייתי","name":false},{"text":"heritage","lemma":"heritage","pos":"noun","he":"מורשת","name":false},{"text":"Snowdonia Slate Trail","lemma":"Snowdonia Slate Trail","pos":"proper noun","he":"שביל הצפחות של סנודוניה","name":true},{"text":"offers","lemma":"offer","pos":"verb","he":"מציע","name":false},{"text":"remarkable","lemma":"remarkable","pos":"adjective","he":"יוצא דופן","name":false},{"text":"journey","lemma":"journey","pos":"noun","he":"מסע","name":false},{"text":"heart","lemma":"heart","pos":"noun","he":"לב","name":false},{"text":"Wales","lemma":"Wales","pos":"proper noun","he":"וויילס","name":true},{"text":"spanning","lemma":"span","pos":"verb","he":"משתרע","name":false},{"text":"miles","lemma":"mile","pos":"noun","he":"מיילים","name":false},{"text":"grand","lemma":"grand","pos":"adjective","he":"גדול","name":false},{"text":"loop","lemma":"loop","pos":"noun","he":"לולאה","name":false},{"text":"national park","lemma":"national park","pos":"noun","he":"פארק לאומי","name":false},{"text":"long-distance","lemma":"long-distance","pos":"adjective","he":"ארוך-טווח","name":false},{"text":"route","lemma":"route","pos":"noun","he":"מסלול","name":false},{"text":"connects","lemma":"connect","pos":"verb","he":"